---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
// Every claim here is documented step by step in the docs' Security group
// (docs/src/content/docs/security/); a change to what a release carries lands there first.
const title = 'Turf — Security: self-hosted, signed, and ready for an air gap';
const description =
  'Turf runs on your machines and keeps your state and credentials there. Every release is signed, ships a software bill of materials, and installs with no route to the internet.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="subhero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Security</p>
      <h1>Turf runs inside your boundary.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Turf™ is self-hosted. The server, the engine and the console run on your
        machines, and your state, plans and credentials stay there. Every release is signed, lists
        what it is built from, and installs on a network with no route to the internet.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Your data stays with you</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No hosted control plane.</strong> There is no Turf service between you and your
            infrastructure. The engine runs beside your workloads, with the credentials you give it.</li>
          <li><strong>No license server and no update check.</strong> Turf runs without calling home
            for either.</li>
          <li><strong>Telemetry you can read and turn off.</strong> Turf sends anonymous usage counts
            by default and never resource contents, credentials, state or names. One command turns
            it off. <a href="/telemetry/">Every field is listed.</a></li>
          <li><strong>A console only you can open.</strong> The console is read-only and asks for a
            token. It loads nothing from outside.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Signed releases, with a bill of materials</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>You can check that a download is the one Turf built, and read exactly what is in it.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Signed build provenance</strong> for every binary and for the container image,
            recorded in the public Sigstore transparency log.</li>
          <li><strong>A software bill of materials</strong> for each component, in CycloneDX and SPDX,
            signed with the release.</li>
          <li><strong>A vulnerability disposition</strong> in OpenVEX: for each known vulnerability in
            a dependency, whether Turf is affected and why.</li>
          <li><strong>Scanned before it ships.</strong> Each release is scanned before it is tagged,
            and its bill of materials is scanned again before it is signed.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The installer verifies the signatures for you with <code>--verify</code>.
          <a href="/docs/security/verify-releases/">Verify a release</a> has every command.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Ready for an air gap</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Turf installs and runs with no route to the internet. Mirror the release and the images,
          serve providers from your own mirror, and set one variable,
          <code>TURF_OFFLINE=1</code>: Turf then makes no call of its own outside your network.</p>
        <p><a href="/docs/security/air-gapped/">Air-gapped installation</a> walks through it and
          lists every host a default installation contacts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Every change is approved</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Approval names the plan.</strong> An approval carries the plan's digest and
            applies that plan only. A plan that changed is refused.
            <a href="/docs/concepts/approval/">How approval works.</a></li>
          <li><strong>Policy reads the plan first.</strong> Your policy tools check each plan before
            it is applied. <a href="/docs/using/policy/">Policy checks.</a></li>
          <li><strong>A credential buys only its tier.</strong> A server started at the read, plan or
            apply tier lists only that tier's tools.
            <a href="/docs/reference/server/#tiers">Tiers.</a></li>
          <li><strong>A record of every run.</strong> The console keeps each round's plan, its
            approver and its result.
            <a href="/docs/concepts/engine-and-console/">The engine and the console.</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Report a vulnerability</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Write to <a href="mailto:security@turf.build">security@turf.build</a>, or use GitHub's
          private vulnerability reporting on the affected repository. We aim to acknowledge a report within
          three business days. The
          <a href="https://github.com/turfbuild/.github/blob/main/SECURITY.md">security policy</a>
          has the details.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
